  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 206 PM CDT Sun Aug 2 2026\n\nAn axis of surface high pressure sits in our area, which is in \nbetween two low pressure systems - one driven by an upper trough \nmoving through the Ohio River Valley region and another driven by an \nupper trough swinging out of Alberta into Saskatchewan. Ridging \nremains dominant over the southwest portion of the CONUS, edging \ninto the Central and Southern Plains. This has led to another day of \npleasant temperatures and relatively low dew points, alongside light \nwinds. We are on track to reach highs in the mid to upper 80s this \nafternoon. With the surface high passing east tonight into Monday, \nperhaps eastern KS could see some patchy fog redevelop in low-lying \nspots, but even so this would be pretty shallow. \n\nHeading into Monday, we return to low-level southerly flow as a lee \ntrough deepens in response to a shortwave digging across the \nNorthern Rockies. This along with increasing mid-level heights will \nallow temperatures to rise each day Monday and Tuesday, bringing \nhighs to the upper 80s to low 90s. There is an outside chance that \nscattered thunderstorms in western KS Monday night could graze our \ncounties in north central KS during the predawn hours Tuesday if \nthey make it that far, but that probability appears low at this \ntime. Better rain chances come to northern KS late Tuesday night as \nthe upper ridge flattens thanks to the northern shortwave, and the \ninverted sfc trough axis makes its way into the area. Ensemble \ntemperature ranges widen by Wednesday, particularly near the I-70 \ncorridor as highs will largely depend on where exactly the frontal \nboundary sets up. In any case there looks to be around a 10-15 \ndegree temperature gradient from north to south across the area. The \nfrontal position will also likely influence where the best storm \nchances will be Wednesday evening/night. Instability and shear would \nsuggest that some strong to severe storms may be possible. By \nThursday, the front looks to be south of the area, limiting high \ntemperatures to the 80s. There still may be enough shortwave energy \nrounding the northeast periphery of the ridge to support another \nround of storms Thursday night, although we'll need to get through \nthe previous round before we really get a handle on how this one \nwill evolve.\n\nBy next weekend, mid-level heights are progged to increase again \nwith the ridge moving back into the area. This favors a warming \ntrend leading to hot and humid conditions once again with highs \nforecast to reach the low to mid 90s. LREF probs for reaching heat \nadvisory criteria are low at this time (10% or less), although they \ndo indicate a 30-50% probability of heat indices reaching 100 \ndegrees on Saturday with a slightly higher chance on Sunday.",
